\s56 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \f0 \b \fs52 {{\NeXTHelpMarker413 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_protocol:NSMenuActionResponder;}
¬}\pard \s56 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql NSMenuActionResponder\
\s30 \li1520 \fi-8 \fs28 (informal protocol)\
\fs20\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s8 \li4233 \fi-2721 \ri1007 \ql \tx4233 \f0 \b \fs24 Category Of:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 NSObject\
\fs20\
\s22 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Declared In:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 AppKit/NSMenu.h \
\fs20\
\fs20\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s55 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Protocol Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 This informal protocol allows your application to update the enabled or disabled status of an NSMenuItem. It declares only one method, \b validateMenuItem:\b0 . By default, every time a user event occurs, NSMenu automatically enables and disables each visible menu item based on criteria described in \'aaAutomatic Updating of NSMenuItems,\'ba below. Implement \b validateMenuItem:\b0  in cases where you want to override NSMenu\'27s default enabling scheme.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSMenuItems can be enabled or disabled in two ways: explicitly, by sending the \b setEnabled:\b0  message, or automatically, as described below. Automatic updating can be turned on and off with NSMenu\'27s \b setAutoenablesItems:\b0  message. \
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Automatic Updating of NSMenuItems\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Whenever a user event occurs, the NSMenu object updates the status of every one of its visible menu items. To update the status of a menu item, an NSMenu tries to find the object that responds to the NSMenuItem\'27s action message. It searches the following objects in order until it finds one that responds to the action message (note that it doesn\'27t actually send the action message):\
\fs14\
\pard \s5 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab The NSMenuItem\'27s target. If the target is non-\b nil\b0 , the search ends here whether the target responds or not.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab The key window\'27s responder chain, starting with its first responder.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab The key window itself.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab The key window\'27s delegate.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab The main window\'27s responder chain, starting with its first responder.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab The main window itself.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab The main window\'27s delegate.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab The NSApplication object.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab The NSApplication object\'27s delegate.\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 If none of these objects responds to the action message, the menu item is disabled. If NSMenu finds an object that responds to the action message, it then checks to see if that object responds to the \b validateMenuItem:\b0  method (the method declared in this informal protocol). If \b validateMenuItem:\b0  is not implemented in that object, the menu item is enabled. If it is implemented, the return value of \b validateMenuItem:\b0  indicates whether the menu item should be enabled or disabled.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Here is an example of using \b validateMenuItem:\b0  to override automatic enabling. If your application has a Copy menu item that sends the \b copy:\b0  action message to the first responder, that menu item is automatically enabled any time an object that responds to \b copy:\b0 , such as an NSText object, is the first responder of the key or main window. If you create a class whose instances might become the first responder, and which doesn\'27t support copying of everything it allows the user to select, you should implement \b validateMenuItem:\b0  in that class. \b validateMenuItem:\b0  will then return NO if items that can\'27t be copied are selected (or if no items are selected) and YES if all items in the selection can be copied. By implementing \b validateMenuItem:\b0 , you can have the Copy menu item disabled even though the target object does implement the \b copy:\b0  method. If your class never permits copying, then you simply omit an implemention of \b copy:\b0  in that class, and the Copy menu item is disabled automatically whenever an instance of that class is the first responder.\
\fs14\
\fs28 If you send a \b setEnabled:\b0  message to enable or disable a menu item when automatic updating is turned on (with NSMenu\'27s \b setAutoEnablesItems:\b0 ), other objects might undo what you have done after another user event occurs. Hence you can never be sure that the menu item will remain the way you set it. If your application must use \b setEnabled:\b0 , turn off the automatic enabling of menu items in order to get predictable results.\
\fs14\
\fs56\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s32 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Instance Methods\
\fs14\
\s41 \li1512 \fi0 \fs28 {{\NeXTHelpMarker4812 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_method:[NSMenuActionResponder-validateMenuItem:];}
¬}\pard \s41 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql validateMenuItem:\
\fs6\
\s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(BOOL)\b validateMenuItem:\b0 (NSMenuItem\'80*)\i aMenuItem\
\fs14\
\i0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Implemented to override the default action of enabling or disabling \i aMenuItem\i0 . The object implementing this method must be the target of \i aMenuItem\i0 .  It returns YES to enable the \i aMenuItem\i0 , NO to disable it. You can determine which menu item \i aMenuItem\i0  is by querying it for its title, tag, or action.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The following example beeps and disables the menu item \'aaNext Record\'ba if the selected line in a table view is the last one; conversely, it beeps and disables the menu item \'aaPrior Record\'ba if the selected row is the first one in the table view. (\b countryKeys\b0  is an array of names appearing in the table view.)\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 - (BOOL)validateMenuItem:(NSMenuItem *)anItem\
\{\
    int row = [tableView selectedRow];\
    if ([[anItem title] isEqualToString:@"Next Record"] &&\
        (row == [countryKeys indexOfObject:[countryKeys lastObject]])) \{\
        NSBeep();\
        return NO;\
    \}\
    if ([[anItem title] isEqualToString:@"Prior Record"] && row == 0 ) \{\
        NSBeep();\
        return NO;\
    \}\
    return YES;\
\}\
\
